[2026-09-29 06:20] Added `make check` as the single verification gate.
why: The chunk rule says nothing is done until it is verified, but verification was five
commands I had to remember in order, and a forgotten one is an unverified chunk. One target that
runs gofmt, vet, race, fixture determinism and a CGO_ENABLED=0 build removes that failure mode
and gives any later session one thing to run.
affects: Makefile

[2026-09-29 06:20] AGENTS.md rewritten as a full session-restore document rather than a status note.
why: It was a progress summary, which is the least useful thing to find at the start of a
session - the expensive knowledge is the environment quirks, the invariants, and the findings
that cost a debugging cycle to learn. It now opens with a ninety-second restore, and carries the
invariants, the hand-off state, the next chunk's sub-steps and the hard-won findings. Because it
is gitignored it can be lost, so the same material is duplicated into this log's CHECKPOINT
blocks, which are committed.
affects: AGENTS.md, DECISIONS.log

[2026-09-29 07:10] M3.1 started: frame decoders. ModeLines added alongside lf and crlf.
why: PRD 5.1 gives TCP the modes lf/crlf/octet/nul/auto, but HTTP's `?framing=lines` is "LF/CRLF" - a third behaviour where \n terminates and a preceding \r is dropped and recorded as CRLF. lf and crlf are strict: in lf mode a lone \r is CONTENT, which is exactly what makes malformed.log's bare-CR records a meaningful test. All four are one delimiter decoder with a policy flag rather than four implementations.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/frame.go

[2026-09-29 07:10] Frame.Raw is a fresh allocation the caller owns, not a view into the read buffer.
why: An aliased buffer would be cheaper, but the stream batcher holds up to 256 frames before calling PutBatch, so views into a reusing buffer are a use-after-free waiting to happen. Framing is not the hot loop - the fsync is - so the copy is not where throughput is won or lost.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/frame.go

[2026-09-29 07:10] Fragmentation happens inside the scan, not as a wrapper around a completed record.
why: Fragmentation exists to bound memory. A decorator that fragments an already-assembled record would have buffered the whole thing first, so a single gigabyte-long line would still take the process down. The delimiter decoder emits a fragment as soon as its window covers max_frame_bytes and it can see that no delimiter starts within it, and the octet decoder streams a long message through without ever holding it whole. TestFragmentsAreBoundedInMemory writes 100 KiB through a 64-byte buffer.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/frame.go

[2026-09-29 07:10] The multiline timeout is the source's job, not the decoder's.
why: PRD 5.2 lists a timeout among the multiline flush triggers, but Next() blocks on the reader, so a decoder physically cannot notice time passing while it waits. Multiline exposes Flush() and the source drives a timer. Putting a timer inside the decoder would have meant a goroutine per connection and a mutex around the accumulator, for a trigger the source already has the clock for.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/multiline.go, pkg/dataplane/ingest/source (M3.2+)

[2026-09-29 07:10] A multiline continuation line with no record in progress is emitted, not discarded.
why: It means the stream did not begin at a record boundary, or the pattern does not match this source. Both are real situations (a tail resuming mid-event, a misconfigured start pattern). Dropping the line would be ingest deciding that data is worthless, which is the one judgement this workstream must never make. Emitting it keeps the round-trip lossless, which TestMultilineIsLossless checks by reconstructing the input from the records plus their terminators.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/multiline.go

[2026-09-29 07:10] A truncated octet message keeps the bytes that arrived, then reports the violation on the next call.
why: The sender declared 20 bytes and delivered 5. Discarding them loses data the vault could have stored. The partial frame is marked FragMore so nothing downstream mistakes it for complete, and the following Next() returns io.ErrUnexpectedEOF so the source can close the connection and count it rather than treating a cut message as a clean end. Standard Go convention: data first, error on the next call.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/frame.go

[2026-09-29 07:12] The framer is checked against the corpus now, not only at the M3.6 e2e gate.
why: testdata/oversize.log and testdata/multiline.log were generated against rules this package has to implement - expected_fragments at max_frame_bytes=1MiB, and one manifest record per multiline event with the internal newlines inside byte_start..byte_end. Asserting that here means a generator/framer disagreement fails with a message naming the rule, rather than surfacing at M3.6 as a mysterious round-trip mismatch in a test that ingests thirteen files at once. Both pass: 3 oversize records, 40 multiline events, byte-exact.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/{fragment_test.go,multiline_test.go}

[2026-09-29 07:12] Detect() is asserted against every fixture, not just hand-written examples.
why: Auto framing is a guess a syslog listener is forced to make, and a wrong guess reads a length out of log text and swallows the connection. No fixture is octet-counted, so Detect must choose line framing for all 13. Checked.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/frame_test.go

[2026-09-29 07:15] M3.1 verified: make check ALL GREEN. FuzzDelim 1.76M execs, FuzzOctet 6.4M execs, both clean.
why: Chunk rule. Chunking invariance is tested for every mode against iotest.OneByteReader, ten fixed chunk sizes, 25 deterministic random chunkings and iotest.DataErrReader (which returns the final bytes together with io.EOF - naive decoders drop the last record on it). FuzzDelim asserts losslessness against arbitrary input rather than examples: Raw plus the recorded terminators must reconstruct the stream byte for byte.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/

[2026-09-29 07:40] Stream batching uses time.AfterFunc, not a goroutine per stream.
why: A stream that goes quiet after one record would otherwise hold it in memory, undurable, until the next arrived - exactly the record a crash loses. A device sending one message a minute must not wait a minute for durability. max_conns is 1024, so a goroutine-per-stream ticker is a thousand goroutines doing nothing; the runtime timer heap already exists.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/ingest.go

[2026-09-29 07:40] BUG FOUND BY THE RACE DETECTOR, worse than a race: concurrent flushes reordered a stream.
why: The batch timer and a full-batch flush both detached the batch and then wrote, so two batches from ONE stream could reach the vault out of the order their records were appended. The visible damage was not subtle - an oversize record's fragment run was split across two out-of-order writes and reassembled into garbage ("record 4 is marked FragCont with nothing to continue"). The race detector separately caught Pipeline.Run closing the output channel while a timer flush was still sending to it.
Fixed by taking the write lock BEFORE detaching the batch, so detach order is write order, and by having Close acquire the same lock so it blocks until any in-flight timer flush finishes. TestFlushesDoNotInterleave reproduces the exact interleaving with a one-nanosecond batch delay and asserts both offsets and vault-assigned RecordIDs are strictly ascending. Clean under -race -count=5.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/ingest.go

[2026-09-29 07:40] A cancelled context during the downstream send reports which record was left unforwarded.
why: The record is already durable and can be replayed from the vault by RecordID, so nothing is lost - but an operator restarting the data plane needs to know where the stream resumes. A bare context error would not say.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/ingest.go

[2026-09-29 07:40] File source resolves every path to an absolute path.
why: Origin.Addr is the de-duplication key for at-least-once tail replay, and a relative path stops meaning the same thing the moment the process changes directory. Globs are expanded at Run so a config can name a directory of logs.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/file.go

[2026-09-29 07:40] File tail mode is accepted by NewFile but Run refuses it explicitly.
why: Config can be written against `mode: tail` today, and the failure is a clear message rather than the source silently behaving like `once` - which would look like it worked and quietly stop following the file. Tail lands later in M3.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/file.go

[2026-09-29 07:40] The e2e round-trip test picks framing from the manifest's terminator field, not a hard-coded list.
why: crlf.log needs CRLF framing and multiline.log needs the multiline pattern. Hard-coding which files are special means a fixture added later gets ingested with the wrong framing by omission and the failure looks like a corpus bug. Reading the manifest makes the corpus itself the source of truth.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/e2e_test.go

[2026-09-29 07:45] M3 GATE MET EARLY: 7598 records across all 13 fixtures round-trip byte-exact.
why: TestRoundTripEveryFixture reads bytes[byte_start:byte_end] straight from each source file and compares against what the vault stored - bytes, SHA-256, terminator, fragment count and origin offset - then requires VerifyChain(deep) to pass. Nothing excluded: malformed.log's invalid UTF-8 and NULs, crlf.log's CRLF, multiline.log's internal newlines, oversize.log's fragments. Three independent computations agree (generator, source file, vault), not one value copied around.
This is the PRD's "Fixture round-trip: 100% identical" success metric, met for the file source. It is NOT yet met end to end through the network sources, which are next.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/

[2026-09-29 07:45] M3.2 verified: make check ALL GREEN, race clean at -count=3.
why: Chunk rule. Pipeline behaviours covered: durability before visibility, batch timer on a quiet stream, backpressure with a 4-slot channel and 200 records, shutdown losing nothing that Submit accepted, vault failure propagating to the source, per-stream ordering across 8 concurrent streams, output channel closed on return.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/

[2026-09-29 08:20] Listeners bind in their constructor, not in Run.
why: A caller needs Addr() before accepting starts - tests bind to port 0 and must know where to dial. Binding in Run would force every test to poll for readiness, and PRD working rule 8 forbids sleep-based synchronisation in tests. The cost is that a bind failure surfaces at construction rather than at Run, which is if anything the better place for it.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/{tcp.go,udp.go,http.go}

[2026-09-29 08:20] At MaxConns a TCP connection is refused immediately rather than queued.
why: Accepting and then stalling looks to the sender like a working connection that is silently losing data. Refusing is the honest answer and the one a syslog sender can act on. Counted so it appears in metrics rather than only in a log.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tcp.go

[2026-09-29 08:20] UDP warns when the kernel clamps SO_RCVBUF.
why: UDP has no backpressure; the receive buffer is the only thing between a burst and silent loss. Asking for 8 MiB and being given 256 KiB without saying so is exactly how UDP loss becomes an unexplained gap in the data weeks later. The effective size is read back with getsockopt and logged with a pointer at net.core.rmem_max. Linux reports double the requested value because it includes bookkeeping overhead; that is reported as-is rather than halved, so the logged number matches what `ss -m` shows.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/udp.go, sockopt_unix.go

[2026-09-29 08:20] A trailing newline on a UDP datagram is content, not a terminator.
why: Nothing framed a datagram - the datagram boundary is the record boundary. A sender that appends "\n" sent 34 bytes, not 33 plus a terminator, and stripping it would break byte-exactness for every such sender. Asserted by TestUDPTrailingNewlineIsContent.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/udp.go

[2026-09-29 08:20] HTTP responds 202 only after every record in the body is durable.
why: A 202 that meant "received" rather than "stored" would invite a client to delete its copy of data that is still only in memory. The stream is flushed before the response is written. TestHTTPResponseMeansDurable scans the vault immediately after the response arrives, with no waiting, and requires every record to be there.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/http.go

[2026-09-29 08:20] A rejected HTTP source_id is never echoed back in the response or the log.
why: It is attacker-controlled and would otherwise land in an operator's terminal and in log aggregation - the same log-injection problem the vaultctl preview escaping solves. The response says what the pattern is instead. Asserted by TestHTTPRejectsBadSourceID.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/http.go

[2026-09-29 08:20] Unconfigured HTTP source ids are refused unless DynamicSources is set.
why: PRD 5.1 makes dynamic_sources opt-in. An endpoint that mints a new source per request is an unbounded metric-label space and an easy way to pollute the corpus with a typo. An explicit AllowedSources list covers the middle ground.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/http.go

[2026-09-29 08:20] Records already stored when an HTTP body fails part-way are NOT rolled back.
why: The vault is append-only and a record that was stored is a record that was received. The failure response reports how many were stored so a client retrying knows it will create duplicates, which Origin.Offset lets a consumer collapse. Rolling back would mean deleting from the vault, which is the one operation it must never have.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/http.go

[2026-09-29 08:25] BUG: shutdown hung when a connection arrived during the shutdown window.
why: The accept loop registered a connection with track() after the shutdown watcher had already walked the connection map and closed everything. That connection was never closed, so its reader blocked until the idle timeout - five minutes by default - and Run never returned. It surfaced as an intermittent hang and only under -race, where the scheduling window is wide enough to hit; it passed at -count=1 and failed at -count=2.
Fixed with a `closing` flag set under the same mutex: track() now refuses after shutdown has begun and the caller closes the connection instead. The accept loop also re-checks ctx. TestTCPShutdownWithConnectionsArriving dials continuously across the cancellation so the window is hit on purpose rather than by luck, with a five-minute idle timeout so a leak hangs the test rather than quietly waiting itself out. Clean at -race -count=4.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tcp.go

[2026-09-29 08:25] M3.3 verified: make check ALL GREEN, race clean at -count=4, 188 subtests in the ingest tree.
why: Chunk rule. TCP (line, auto, octet framing; slow clients; 12 concurrent connections keeping per-connection order; max_conns; idle timeout; framing errors closing the connection), UDP (datagram boundaries, binary payloads, trailing newline as content), HTTP (lines/whole/octet framing, chunked bodies, 202-means-durable, source id validation, 413, 503 on an unhealthy vault, a fixture round-trip through curl-shaped requests).
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/

[2026-09-29 08:50] Config rejects keys for features that are not implemented yet.
why: PRD section 8 lists vault.compact, compact_block_bytes, zstd_level, hash_index and peer_map. None of them exist yet. A config struct that accepted and ignored them would leave an operator certain compaction was running - the worst possible failure, because it looks like success. Each key arrives with its implementation, and until then `compact: true` is a startup error naming the unknown key.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/config.go

[2026-09-29 08:50] Fields belonging to another source type are a config error, not ignored.
why: `paths` on a UDP listener, `listen` on a file source, `framing` on HTTP (where it is chosen per request by ?framing=), `cert` on plain TCP. Silently ignoring any of them is how someone ends up certain a file is being read when nothing is reading it. Every mismatch is named.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/config.go

[2026-09-29 08:50] sync: none requires ULPF_ALLOW_SYNC_NONE=1.
why: In that mode an acknowledgement stops meaning the record is on disk, which silently voids the central durability claim. It is legitimate for benchmarks, so it is not removed - but it must never be reached by editing one word in a config. The error says "acknowledged records are NOT durable" rather than just refusing.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/config.go

[2026-09-29 08:50] Config validation reports every error at once, via errors.Join.
why: An operator fixing a config one restart at a time is an operator wasting an afternoon. TestAllErrorsAreReportedAtOnce asserts a config with three separate mistakes names all three.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/config.go

[2026-09-29 08:55] `ingestd --emit` base64-encodes the payload.
why: A raw log line is attacker-controlled. Printing it unescaped into a terminal or a JSON Lines file that a log aggregator will parse is log injection - the same problem vaultctl's preview escaping solves. The JSON carries raw_base64 and raw_bytes, so a consumer gets the exact bytes without anything interpreting them on the way.
affects: cmd/ingestd/main.go

[2026-09-29 08:55] A shutdown that times out exits 1, not 0.
why: It may have left records accepted but not yet durable. A supervisor that treated a timed-out shutdown as a clean stop would hide exactly the condition the durability claim is about.
affects: cmd/ingestd/main.go

[2026-09-29 08:55] `type: tls` is refused with an explicit error rather than served as plaintext.
why: TLS listeners land in M4. A config naming type tls that quietly served unencrypted traffic would be a security failure disguised as a working deployment. Asserted by TestTLSIsRefusedRatherThanIgnored.
affects: cmd/ingestd/main.go

[2026-09-29 08:55] `--once` cancels the context rather than having its own exit path.
why: Network listeners never finish on their own, so --once has to stop them. Cancelling means shutdown runs through exactly the same code a SIGTERM takes, so the drain-and-seal path is exercised by every CI run rather than only in production. --once with no `mode: once` source is an error, because it would otherwise hang forever.
affects: cmd/ingestd/main.go

[2026-09-29 09:00] BUG: a glob matching no files was treated as a missing file and failed the source.
why: expand() kept an unmatched pattern as a literal path so that a typo would be reported by name. That is right for a literal path - a typo must not look like a source that is healthily reading zero records - but wrong for a pattern: an empty log directory is normal, and in tail mode files appear later. Now a path containing *, ? or [ that matches nothing logs a warning and is skipped, while a literal path that does not exist still fails by name. Found by TestSecondDaemonOnOneVaultIsRefused, which used an intentionally-empty glob.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/file.go

[2026-09-29 09:00] Test harness caches the pipeline's exit instead of exposing a bare channel.
why: A test that read h.done directly consumed the value h.stop() was also waiting for, so cleanup blocked for ten seconds and reported "the pipeline did not shut down" - a confusing failure caused by the harness, not by the code under test. waitRun() is idempotent and both paths use it.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/net_test.go

[2026-09-29 09:05] M3.4 and M3.5 verified end to end against a live daemon.
why: Chunk rule, plus the smoke test from PRD 11.5 run for real. Started ingestd with the dev config and fed it all five paths: healthz 200, a UDP datagram, two TCP lines, one octet-counted TCP message (30 bytes, decoded back exactly), and the 50-line sample ASA file over HTTP (202 {"accepted":50}). SIGTERM exited 0. The resulting vault: 2 sealed segments, 2144 records, `verify --deep` exit 0, a proof verified standalone through verify-proof, and `get --raw | shasum` matching the manifest's expected_sha256 for that record.
First third-party dependency added: gopkg.in/yaml.v3 (config), pinned at v3.0.1.
affects: cmd/ingestd/, configs/ingest.dev.yaml, pkg/dataplane/ingest/config.go

[2026-09-29 09:15] File tail is polling-based; fsnotify is deliberately NOT added.
why: PRD 5.1 specifies fsnotify with a 500ms polling fallback, and fsnotify is pre-approved in PRD section 10. Implementing polling only is a deviation, taken for three reasons. First, the fallback has to exist and be correct regardless - PRD 11.4 says bind mounts break fsnotify, so containers run `poll: true` anyway, which is where this will actually be deployed. Second, the benefit is latency, and 500ms is far below anything that matters here: the vault's seal interval is 5-30s and nothing downstream is waiting on a single line. Third, it keeps the dependency count at one, which is worth something for the air-gap build. The polling loop is isolated behind one function, so adding fsnotify later as an accelerator is a contained change rather than a rewrite. Revisit if a measured requirement appears.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tail.go, go.mod

[2026-09-29 09:15] Tail checkpoints pulled forward from M4 into M3, alongside tail itself.
why: Same reasoning as the record codec at M1. Tail without resume is not a feature anyone would ship, and building it twice - once without checkpoints and once with - means the first version's rotation and truncation handling gets rewritten rather than reused. The two are one coherent piece.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tail.go, M4 scope

[2026-09-29 09:40] BUG: appending to a file changed its fingerprint, so checkpoints never matched on small files.
why: The fingerprint was the SHA-256 of the first min(1024, size) bytes. For a file under 1024 bytes that covers the WHOLE file, so every append changed it and every resume decided it was looking at a different file and re-read from zero. A log file is append-only, so its first N bytes are immutable once it is N bytes long - the fix is to store fingerprint_len alongside the fingerprint and recompute over exactly that prefix rather than over however much is there now. Caught by TestTailResumesFromCheckpoint, which read "one" again instead of "four".
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/checkpoint.go, tail.go

[2026-09-29 09:40] Checkpoint file names are a SHA-256 of the log path, not the path itself.
why: A log path can contain slashes, spaces, dots and anything else a filesystem allows. Building a checkpoint file name out of it is how directory traversal happens, and this is one of the few places in the workstream where an external string could reach a filesystem path. Asserted by TestCheckpointNamesAreHashed.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/checkpoint.go

[2026-09-29 09:40] The tail reader never returns EOF; it waits.
why: A framer that saw EOF at the end of a file would emit a half-written line as a complete record and the rest as a second one, splitting an event in two at exactly the moment a writer was mid-write. Handing the framer a stream that does not end means a partial line is held until its terminator arrives, which is what TestTailHoldsAPartialLine checks by writing "half a li", waiting several poll intervals, and requiring zero records.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tail.go

[2026-09-29 09:40] A tail waits for a file that does not exist rather than failing.
why: A file named explicitly may not be there yet, or may be momentarily absent between a rename and a create. Failing would make an ordinary log rotation take the source down. A literal path that never appears is visible as a source producing nothing, and the warning says so.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tail.go

[2026-09-29 09:40] checkpoint_dir, checkpoint_every and poll are config errors on a non-tail file source.
why: Same rule as the rest of the config: accepting them on a one-shot read and ignoring them would suggest a restart resumes, when in fact it re-reads the whole file every time.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/config.go

[2026-09-29 09:45] M3 COMPLETE. make check ALL GREEN, race clean at -count=2, 2812 passing subtests.
why: Chunk rule. All six sub-chunks done: frame decoders, file source (once and tail), TCP/UDP/HTTP, Pipeline, config + ingestd, and the e2e byte-exact round-trip gate (7598 records across 13 fixtures). Tail covers appends, partial lines held until terminated, rename-and-create rotation, copy-truncate truncation, from: end, waiting for a file to appear, checkpoint resume, at-least-once ordering, stale and corrupt checkpoints, and multiple files concurrently.
Still outstanding for M4: TLS listeners (currently refused explicitly), the format sniff hint (every RawEvent carries HintUnknown), peer-CIDR to source_id mapping, the multiline timeout being driven by the source, and the Prometheus registry. M5: compaction, the 200-cycle kill -9 suite, benchmarks, docs/vault-format.md.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/, cmd/ingestd/

